Mr. PinPin licensing scope

Copyright (c) 2026 Miguel Lemos, for copyrightable contributions he owns.

This is a mixed software and creative-content project, NOT a blanket MIT release.

The standard MIT grant in LICENSE-MIT applies only to project-owned functional
software and its technical documentation: for example reader/navigation logic,
rendering utilities, build/test/storage/publishing tools, and deployment code.
It applies only to rights Miguel Lemos owns or is authorized to license.
Third-party code remains under its own license, not this project's MIT grant.

Specifically excluded pending separate clearance: docs/storyboard/gpu/**, whose
SOURCE.md records adaptation from GauchoAI/gaucho-atlas without an established
upstream license. The complete preserved original export (docs/original.html and
docs/images/**) is also excluded. In a deployed archive these paths omit docs/.
Character-specific procedural artwork, geometry, and creative configuration
(including in scripts/blender-pinpin/**) are content, not automatically MIT code.

Excluded from that grant are stories, translations, illustrations, character
designs, audio, video, models, prompts, reference material, provenance creative
content, and other creative content. These exclusions apply even when content
is embedded in JS, JSON, HTML, CSS, code comments, tests, or documentation.
A filename, extension, directory, content hash, or public download is not a
license decision. In mixed files, separate functional software from creative
content; the MIT grant covers only the former where project-owned.

Creative material in deployed releases, release archives, and the public HF
bucket is also excluded from MIT. Release manifests do not relicense what they
describe. See CONTENT-LICENSE.md for limited personal/family permissions and
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md for upstream terms and unresolved ownership boundaries.

This notice does not claim copyright in public-domain material, facts, ideas,
or purely AI-generated material lacking copyright protection under applicable
law. Copyrightable human contributions, if any, are considered separately.
Fair use, fair dealing, other legal exceptions, and valid prior grants remain
unaffected. No trademark, endorsement, or personality-rights permission is
implied. Preserve relevant notices when copying covered software or content.
